> Thus raising monster difficulty without gaining any equipment.

Because after all orcs, gnomes, dwarves, elves, centaurs,
trolls and so on are never generated with equipment you might
want given the XP and depth you're currently at.  And a living
pet can never tell you which of them are cursed so even that
nonexistant equipment is worthless.

There are levels were few items are to be gained, but even
with only death drops that are too rare to depend on in the
early levels there's still the fact that since monster difficulty
is based on (XP+depth)/2 they still get meaner at half the
rate you do.  Plus hanging out gives your pet more time to
gain hit points.

It all adds up to if a level is not currently generating monsters
I'm not going to stay to see if it does, but as long as the rate
of random generation stays high enough that I am likely to
gain XP or items relative to my current status I see point in
continuing to use the local resources.  Give me a good
artifact weapon and a reasonable armor suite and random
generation is extremely unlikely to give me sufficient benefit
but in the early levels where orcs, dwarves, gnomes and
elves are still likely to give me better equipment than I have
there's point in staying.

And so there are some games I'll descend to depth N while
still XP N-1 (N one digit number) and some games I move
up several XP levels because an abundance of
orcs/dwarves/elves/whatever happen to show up to give me
daggers or cloaks or whatever.  Having lots of elves and
hobbits drop elven daggers doesn't suck in most games.


